+# Security Release Notes — BlueZ (CVE-2023-51594)
+
+## Overview
+
+This note summarizes the **CVE-2023-51594** fix applied to this BlueZ build for client and compliance reference.
+
+---
+
+## CVE-2023-51594 — BlueZ OBEX out-of-bounds read
+
+- **Component:** BlueZ OBEX library (`gobex/gobex.c`, function `g_obex_setpath`).
+- **Issue:** Out-of-bounds read (CWE-125) due to insufficient validation of the OBEX SetPath path parameter. The code could read past the end of the path buffer when the path was shorter than expected.
+- **Impact:** Information disclosure. Attack requires a malicious Bluetooth device and user interaction (target must connect to that device). No public PoC or exploit; no evidence of exploitation in the wild.
+- **Upstream fix:** Commit `1764cea5c7fd4f4a7af06c183822158c1e4c6fe7` (Bastien Nocera) — adds `strlen(path) >= 2` before using `path` in SetPath handling.
+
+### What we did
+
+- Applied the upstream fix (backport of the above commit) to the BlueZ OBEX code path. This removes the out-of-bounds read in `g_obex_setpath()` for short or malformed path values.
+
+### Official / distro status
+
+- **NVD and ZDI** do not publicly map CVE-2023-51594 to this exact commit. The CVE is described at a high level ("lack of proper validation of OBEX protocol parameters").
+- **Debian and Ubuntu** still list the stock BlueZ package (e.g. 5.55-3.1+deb11u2) as **vulnerable** for this CVE; the fix has not been adopted in the official Bullseye package at the time of this note.
+- This build includes a **voluntary backport** of the upstream fix. For your **patched build**, you can treat CVE-2023-51594 as **mitigated**. For strict compliance (e.g. "fixed" vs "vulnerable" by package version), the distro tracker remains the usual source of truth.
+
+### Containers and risk
+
+- **Typical containers:** No Bluetooth stack, no OBEX daemon — **risk is effectively zero** unless you explicitly expose Bluetooth and run OBEX.
+- **If Bluetooth/OBEX are exposed** (e.g. device passthrough, D-Bus, obexd running), the CVE is relevant; this patch is then the appropriate mitigation.
+
+### Recommendation
+
+- **Treat this patched build as mitigated** for CVE-2023-51594.
+- No public reproduction steps or PoC exist; exploitability is low (proximity, user interaction, no known exploit code).

Description: CVE-2023-51594 - Fix buffer overrun in obexd
 Don't access path at byte 2 when it might only contain a single byte.
 The issue results from the lack of proper validation of user-supplied
 data, which can result in a read past the end of an allocated buffer.
Origin: upstream, commit:1764cea5c7fd4f4a7af06c183822158c1e4c6fe7
Author: Bastien Nocera
Last-Update: 2024-06-03

--- a/gobex/gobex.c
+++ b/gobex/gobex.c
@@ -1610,7 +1610,7 @@ guint g_obex_setpath(GObex *obex, const char *path, GObexResponseFunc func,

 	memset(&data, 0, sizeof(data));

-	if (path != NULL && strncmp("..", path, 2) == 0) {
+	if (path != NULL && strlen(path) >= 2 && strncmp("..", path, 2) == 0) {
 		data.flags = 0x03;
 		folder = (path[2] == '/') ? &path[3] : NULL;
 	} else {
